# Context Discipline (Token Efficiency)

## Command output — always byte-cap

When executing shell commands that may produce large output, the `ShellTool` automatically caps output at 4,000 bytes. If writing commands directly, use byte caps:

```bash
# Never: unbounded output
grep -r "pattern" .

# Always: byte-capped
grep -rn "pattern" . 2>&1 | head -c 4000
```

## Targeted reads — never dump full files

| Do | Don't |
|----|-------|
| `read(file, offset, limit)` | `read(file)` for large files |
| `grep("pattern", path)` first | Open a file you haven't searched first |
| Read slices (60-200 lines) | Read files > 500 lines whole |
| Reference existing symbols via grep | Re-read the whole project structure |

## Context levels (Multi-Resolution Pipeline)

The worker receives context at one of 4 levels:

| Level | What's included | Token budget | When |
|-------|----------------|-------------|------|
| 0 | Task description only | ~50 | Typos, renames, simple syntax fixes |
| 1 | + Module overview | ~150 | Low complexity tasks |
| 2 | + Function signatures | ~500 | Medium complexity (default) |
| 3 | + Code chunks + file | ~1,200 | High complexity, new features |

`suggest_context_level(task)` selects the level automatically based on task complexity.
